---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Architecture — sum">
  <div class="doc">
    <h1>Architecture</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Roles, state, and what ships in the distro. Instructions and skills do
      the reasoning. HC owns terminal processes; Git owns worktrees. A small synchronous
      helper preserves task records. A scoped non-agent event observer reconciles the ledger. There is no task scheduler, database server, or permanent hierarchy of managers.
    </p>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2 style="margin:0 0 14px">Session roles</h2>
      <div class="cells roles" style="margin-bottom:14px">
        <div>
          <div
            style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:21px;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:6px"
          >
            coordinator
          </div>
          <div style="color:var(--body);font-size:12px">
            An explicit qualified HC launch binds the coordinator and its initialization receipt; no implicit claim from a working directory.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div
            style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:21px;margin-bottom:6px"
          >
            worker
          </div>
          <div style="color:var(--body);font-size:12px">
            A pane dispatched by the coordinator is registered as that task's
            worker and stays a worker even when its task is editing sum itself.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div
            style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:21px;margin-bottom:6px"
          >
            developer
          </div>
          <div style="color:var(--body);font-size:12px">
            Other sessions default to developer, cannot dispatch or rebind tasks, and modify Sum only in an isolated development checkout.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Native identity is permanent core ID and returned terminal address, with creation time, task/role/attempt/generation, process and matching initialization receipt for Sum authority. Names, labels, sockets, cwd, PID and status alone confer none. <code>doctor</code> only observes and never starts a core or binds a role.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>State and communication</h2>
      <p>
        Private state lives in <code>.sum/</code> and is ignored by Git. Each
        task stores its brief, base SHA, branch/worktree, pane bindings,
        questions, answers, report, an append-only evidence list, the bound
        reviewer endpoint, the last reconciled PR identity, and a versions
        sidecar with brief revisions. File updates are locked and atomically
        replaced on one local machine. Records-only backups import through <code>restore ARCHIVE NEW_HOME</code> into a fresh write-blocked quarantine. Saved reads preserve exact task/procedure history; no coordinator, session or native runtime authority transfers, and direct extraction is not a writable restore.
      </p>
      <p>
        Workers use the exact commands in their generated brief. The core
        interaction is:
      </p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div>./bin/sumctl inbox --live</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl show TASK_ID</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl answer TASK_ID QUESTION_ID --text 'Keep both endpoints for one release.'</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin:14px 0 12px">
        <code>show</code> returns the whole record and keeps its shape. <code
          >context</code
        > is the bounded read beside it:
      </p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div>./bin/sumctl context TASK_ID                              <span class="c"># outline: counts, outstanding decisions, latest handoff, cursor</span></div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl context TASK_ID --role worker|reviewer|coordinator</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl context TASK_ID --section decisions --after 20 --limit 20</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl context TASK_ID --section brief --revision r1 <span class="c"># hash-verified, never rewritten</span></div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl context TASK_ID --since CURSOR</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl notes TASK_ID --text 'finding'</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl help [TOPIC]</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Sections: outline, brief, decisions, handoff, evidence, execution,
        environment, update, returns, notes. Worker prose, handoffs, and notes
        are labelled claims; only <code>verify</code> and <code
          >pr reconcile</code
        > records are verification evidence. Nothing here calls a model or reads
        a worker's checkout.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>Questions and returns</h2>
      <p>A question is saved before notification. Qualified Codex 0.166.0 defers working/blocked/unknown, guards idle with <code>if:idle</code>, and sends observed Done without <code>if</code> or <code>force</code>. A raced busy turn queues Tab for the next turn; this deliberately relaxed Done guard is not atomic. Send success means submitted, not applied.</p>
      <p>
        Every pending return is derived from the records themselves: an open
        question, an unverified report, and a recorded review verdict are owed
        to the parent, an unapplied answer and a requested brief revision to the
        worker. Nothing has to be acknowledged for it to stay listed, and
        nothing but a later record closes it.
      </p>
      <p>The scoped observer performs bounded native-event and snapshot reconciliation; an enabled preference alone proves no running observer. Submitted or uncertain mutations are never automatically replayed. Native idle/Done is not completion and a report still needs independent review and coordinator verification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>Three command classes</h2>
      <div class="kv">
        <div class="k">read-only</div>
        <div class="v">
          status, inbox, --live, show, context, doctor. Write nothing under the
          state home; never call GitHub.
        </div>
        <div class="k">fast delivery</div>
        <div class="v">
          init, bind, pump, notice, observer events. Budgeted delivery passes
          (default 20 s); never call GitHub or apply cleanup.
        </div>
        <div class="k">maintenance</div>
        <div class="v">
          pr reconcile, pipeline, cleanup [--apply], sweep. Explicit; one PR
          observation per recorded open PR, guarded cleanup, 60 s admission
          budget.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sec">
      <h2>Manual dispatch</h2>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div>cp templates/task.md /tmp/my-task.md</div>
        <div class="c"># Edit the brief, then from inside a HC terminal:</div>
        <div>./bin/sumctl dispatch --repo /absolute/path/to/repo \</div>
        <div>&#32;&#32;--brief /tmp/my-task.md --harness codex --approved</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>--approved</code> records the caller's assertion of approval; it
        is not a security boundary. <code>prepare</code> creates the
        record/worktree without launching; <code>start TASK_ID</code> starts
        that prepared task once. The helper is called sumctl to avoid shadowing
        the Unix sum command. Normally you talk to the harness, not the helper.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
